Title:
Offensive COM (Component Object Model)

Type:
Documentation

Short Summary (4-8 sentences max):
- This repository is a structured knowledge base on Windows COM/DCOM offensive security, intended to take readers from COM fundamentals to exploitation and zero-day research workflows.  
- It organizes COM attack surface into chapters covering DCOM lateral movement, COM-based privilege escalation, UAC bypass via elevated COM objects/monikers, COM persistence via registry/TypeLib hijacking, and execution/defense-evasion patterns.  
- The material emphasizes practical operator details (CLSID/AppID paths, activation mechanics, marshaling/OBJREF, impersonation pitfalls) and includes per-technique OpSec/detection/mitigation guidance plus diagrams.  
- It also includes “Research Lab” sections that outline repeatable methodologies for enumeration, triage, reverse engineering, harnessing, and fuzzing COM marshal paths to find new vulnerabilities.  
- Useful for red teamers/pentesters needing COM tradecraft, malware developers building stealthy persistence/execution, and blue teams/detection engineers mapping telemetry and hardening controls.  
- Notably, the README warns the content is AI-generated and may be inaccurate, so technical claims should be validated in a lab.

Technical Focus:
- COM internals: activation, apartments, marshaling (OBJREF/NDR), registry anatomy (HKCR/HKCU shadowing)
- DCOM remoting and lateral movement primitives (MMC20.Application, ShellWindows, Office automation)
- COM privilege escalation via impersonation and “Potato” family techniques
- UAC bypass via elevation moniker/auto-elevation and elevated COM interfaces (e.g., IFileOperation, ICMLuaUtil)
- COM persistence: per-user COM hijack, TreatAs redirection, TypeLib hijacking, scheduled task ComHandler
- Detection engineering for COM abuse (Sysmon/Security/ETW mapping, hardening like KB5004442)

Use Cases:
- Build/validate COM/DCOM lateral movement playbooks and prerequisites per target build
- Identify and exploit COM misconfigurations leading to LPE or token impersonation abuse
- Implement UAC bypass chains leveraging auto-elevated COM objects/monikers
- Establish persistence via HKCU COM hijacking, TreatAs/TypeLib redirection, or ComHandler tasks
- Create detection rules and telemetry coverage for COM-based tradecraft (including ETW/Sysmon)
- Stand up a COM vulnerability research pipeline (enumeration → RE → fuzzing → reporting)

Keywords:
Windows COM, DCOM, CLSID, AppID, TypeLib, HKCR, HKCU shadowing, COM hijacking, TreatAs, elevation moniker, auto-elevation, UAC bypass, IFileOperation, ICMLuaUtil, MMC20.Application, ShellWindows, marshaling, OBJREF, NDR, impersonation, RottenPotato, JuicyPotato, RoguePotato, PrintSpoofer, GodPotato, KB5004442, Sysmon, ETW, AMSI, Squiblydoo, DotNetToJScript, ComHandler, PPL injection, NTLM relay, MSRC, ZDI